    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The cross-referencing helps, but it hasn't yielded a clear \"smoking gun\" yet. It's almost like the errors are hiding in plain sight—they're too small to trigger an automatic red flag in our compliance software, but they're definitely there. It's quite frustrating because they don't seem to follow any logical administrative error pattern like a typo or a duplicate invoice. \n\nWhat other specific methods can I use to ferret out these little anomalies if they're intentionally being kept under the radar?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the anomalies are deliberately being kept under the radar, you'll need to look at the *behavior* around the transactions, not just the numbers themselves. Try to map out the personnel who authorized or processed those specific transactions during those recurring timeframes. This requires looking at the approval chain—who signed off on the expense when the small, suspicious purchase was made? Sometimes, the pattern isn't in the supply, but in the *person* who approved the small, seemingly insignificant purchase.",
